"comparison": "Both GS and Citi disclose the comprehensive composition and accounting treatment of their Allowance for Credit Losses (ACL) for assets at amortized cost. GS describes classifying loans into wholesale and consumer portfolios to measure the allowance collectively using modeled approaches or asset-specific bases. Citi provides a broader definition of its ACL, explicitly including not only funded loans (ACLL) but also reserves for unfunded lending commitments (ACLUC) and other financial assets like held-to-maturity securities, all of which are reflected in the Provision for credit losses. While GS focuses on the portfolio segmentation for measurement methodology, Citi clarifies that the ACL encompasses a wider range of financial instruments and off-balance sheet commitments.",
